@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer

@* FR-68: one conversation in the dispatch desk's roster.

   Its own component rather than a block inside the screen's @foreach, and the reason is testable
   rendering: the roster arrives over a live hub connection, so a statically rendered screen never
   holds a row and nothing in this solution could read one back. Given its summary as a parameter,
   this renders on its own (DW-17, DW-18) - which is what makes the name, the vehicle, the plate,
   the duty badge and `aria-current` assertable at all.

   A bare <button> rather than a `.btn`: this is not an action on a record, it is a row of a list
   that happens to be operable, and every framework button treatment fights the three stacked facts
   inside it.

   Independent @if blocks and one literal localizer key per string, for the reason the screens
   state: an `else` at markup level reads to NFR-14's scan as a rendered English word. *@

<li>
    @* aria-current on the open one, decided in ChatViews rather than here. It is the only thing
       that tells a screen-reader user which of twelve conversations is the one on the right of the
       card - the fill and the edge bar that say so to everyone else are invisible to them. *@
    <button type="button"
            class="dt-chat-thread"
            aria-current="@(ChatViews.OpenThreadMark(Summary!.DriverId, OpenDriverId))"
            @onclick="OnOpen">
        <span class="dt-chat-thread__name">@Summary.DriverName</span>

        @* What the chat service had already loaded and was throwing away. Two drivers can share a
           display name; the van and the plate are what tell them apart, and the duty badge is what
           says whether the person being written to is at the wheel at all. Absent means absent - a
           driver with no vehicle gets no blank line where one would be. *@
        @if (Summary.VehicleModel is not null)
        {
            <span class="dt-chat-thread__vehicle">@Summary.VehicleModel</span>
        }
        @if (Summary.VehicleLicensePlate is not null)
        {
            <span class="dt-chat-thread__plate">@Summary.VehicleLicensePlate</span>
        }
        @if (Summary.OnDuty)
        {
            <DtBadge Class="dt-chat-thread__duty">@Localizer["ShiftOpen"]</DtBadge>
        }
        @* The off state wears the muted class rather than a colour of its own, which is how the
           design draws the absence of something. *@
        @if (!Summary.OnDuty)
        {
            <DtBadge Class="dt-chat-thread__duty dt-muted">@Localizer["ShiftClosed"]</DtBadge>
        }
    </button>
</li>

@code {
    /// <summary>The roster row this renders.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public ChatThreadSummaryView? Summary { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The conversation currently on screen, or null when none is open. A plain number because that
    /// is what crosses the interop boundary; nothing authorizes on it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int? OpenDriverId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What opening this conversation does. A <see cref="Func{TResult}"/> rather than an
    /// <c>EventCallback</c> so the screen can hand over the handler it already builds in C# — a
    /// <c>=&gt;</c> written in an attribute closes the tag as far as NFR-14's markup scan is
    /// concerned, and everything after it is read as rendered English.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Non-null with a default that does nothing, because <c>@@onclick</c> hands this straight to
    /// <c>EventCallback.Factory</c> and a null delegate there is a warning the build treats as an
    /// error. A row that was given no handler is a row that does nothing when pressed, which is the
    /// honest reading of not being given one.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public Func<Task> OnOpen { get; set; } = () => Task.CompletedTask;
}
